\title{The Filter Cap Law:\\ $4/3$ for Exchangeable Dials, $1/\theta$ for Structural Ones}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{28 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete analysis of a one-parameter cost model for candidate filters
(\emph{dials}) in trial-division search, and use it to separate two regimes that are
routinely conflated.  Normalising the cost of an unfiltered sweep to $1$, a dial with
retention $\theta\in(0,1]$ and soundness $s\in[0,1]$ has expected cost $1-s+s\theta$ and
speedup $(1-s+s\theta)^{-1}$.  We prove: (i) an \emph{exchangeable} dial ($s=\theta$) has
speedup at most $4/3$, with equality exactly at $\theta=1/2$; (ii) a dial exceeds $4/3$
if and only if $s(1-\theta)>1/4$, which exchangeability makes impossible because
$\theta(1-\theta)\le 1/4$; (iii) any dial beating the cap satisfies
$s-\theta > (1-2\theta)^2/\bigl(4(1-\theta)\bigr)$, a bound vanishing exactly at
$\theta=1/2$, which identifies half-retention as the extremal test point; (iv) a
\emph{deterministic} dial ($s=1$) has speedup $1/\theta$, and the family of wheel dials
of squarefree modulus $M$, with speedup $M/\varphi(M)$, is unbounded by divergence of
$\sum 1/p$; (v) the residue table revealed by the Berggren tree of Pythagorean triples is
the fixed two-element set $\{(1,0,1),(3,0,1)\}$ modulo $4$, identical for every target,
so the dial it induces carries mutual information exactly $0$ with the target --- the
absolute floor --- while reading a speedup of $2>4/3$; and (vi) a fixed dial against a
\emph{uniform} prior has soundness equal to retention and is therefore capped, whereas
against a prior \emph{supported} in its kept set it has soundness $1$.  The escape is
created by the prior, never by information.  Consequently the $4/3$ cap is not a bound on
speedups: it bounds dials with no answer-bias, and a blind structural exclusion supplies
such a bias for free, so a reading above $4/3$ is never by itself evidence of instance
information.  In logarithmic coordinates the two
regimes are visibly different objects --- structural weights are additive under
composition and unbounded, while exchangeable weights are confined to the window
$[0,\log(4/3)]$ and closed under composition.
\end{abstract}

