\section{Introduction}

A search over a candidate set is the most primitive algorithm there is; filtering that set
before searching is the most primitive optimisation.  This paper answers: how much is a
filter worth, and where does its worth come from?

The naive intuition is that a filter's value is measured by how much it discards.  This is
false, quantitatively and interestingly.  A filter that discards $99\%$ of candidates but
discards the answer $99\%$ of the time is worth almost nothing: the $1\%$ of the time it
succeeds it saves a full sweep, and the other $99\%$ one pays for the filtered sweep
\emph{and} the full sweep.  There is an exact tension between aggressiveness and
reliability, and once written down it yields a hard ceiling of $4/3$.

The second intuition --- that a filter's value tracks the \emph{information} it carries
about the instance --- is also false, and more interestingly so.  We exhibit a filter with
mutual information exactly zero against the instance that nonetheless outperforms the
ceiling binding every unbiased filter.  Resolving that apparent contradiction
is the substance of the paper.

\paragraph{Setting.}
Throughout, the object sought is a nontrivial divisor $p$ of an odd composite $N$, found
by descending trial division from $\lfloor\sqrt{N}\rfloor$.  Nothing depends on this
application; the reader may substitute any search whose baseline is an unfiltered sweep.
What we do use, in Section~\ref{sec:dichotomy}, is a structural fact about the search:
\emph{the divisors of an odd number are odd}.  This is a statement about the problem, not
about any instance, and it is exactly the leverage the structural regime exploits.

\section{The cost model}

\begin{definition}[Dial]
A \emph{dial} is a rule applied to the candidate pool of a search, characterised by its
\emph{retention} $\theta\in(0,1]$, the fraction of candidates it retains, and its
\emph{soundness} $s\in[0,1]$, the probability that the sought object is among the retained
candidates.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Cost and speedup]
Normalise the cost of an unfiltered sweep to $1$.  The \emph{expected cost} of a dial is
\[
  \cost(s,\theta) \;=\; s\theta + (1-s)\cdot 1 \;=\; 1 - s + s\theta,
\]
and its \emph{speedup} is $\spd(s,\theta) = \cost(s,\theta)^{-1}$.
\end{definition}

The derivation is the two-branch accounting: sweep the retained fraction at cost $\theta$;
with probability $s$ the object is found and the search terminates; with probability
$1-s$ the complement must be swept as well, for total cost $\theta+(1-\theta)=1$.  Note
$\cost(1,\theta)=\theta$ and $\cost(0,\theta)=1$.

\begin{lemma}[Positivity]\label{lem:pos}
For $0\le s\le 1$ and $\theta>0$ we have $\cost(s,\theta)>0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $s=1$ the cost is $\theta>0$.  If $s<1$ then $1-s>0$ and $s\theta\ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Exchangeable dial]
A dial is \emph{exchangeable} if $s=\theta$: it retains the sought object with exactly the
probability with which it retains an arbitrary candidate.
\end{definition}

Exchangeability formalises ``the filter has no special insight into where the answer is.''
It is a statement about the joint behaviour of filter and answer, not about the filter
alone: the same filter may be exchangeable against one prior and perfectly sound against
another.  Section~\ref{sec:dichotomy} shows this is the whole story.  For an exchangeable
dial, $\cost(\theta,\theta) = 1-\theta+\theta^2$.

\section{The cap law}

\begin{lemma}[Exchangeable cost floor]\label{lem:floor}
For all $\theta\in\R$, $\cost(\theta,\theta)\ge 3/4$, with equality iff $\theta = 1/2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$1-\theta+\theta^2-\tfrac34 = \bigl(\theta-\tfrac12\bigr)^2 \ge 0$, with equality exactly
at $\theta=1/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The cap law]\label{thm:cap}
Every exchangeable dial with retention $\theta\in(0,1]$ satisfies
$\spd(\theta,\theta)\le 4/3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The cost is positive (Lemma~\ref{lem:pos}) and at least $3/4$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:floor});
inverting reverses the inequality and $(3/4)^{-1}=4/3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
There is no $\theta\in(0,1]$ with $\spd(\theta,\theta)>4/3$: the barrier has no
counterexample.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Extremality of half-retention]\label{thm:extremal}
For $\theta\in(0,1]$, $\spd(\theta,\theta) = 4/3$ if and only if $\theta=1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equality of speedups forces $\cost(\theta,\theta)=3/4$, hence $(\theta-1/2)^2=0$.
Conversely $1-\tfrac12+\tfrac14=\tfrac34$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal} is the design principle for any test of the cap: at
$\theta=1/2$ the exchangeable dial sits exactly on the boundary, so any real bias toward
the answer registers immediately.  Elsewhere the exchangeable value is strictly below
$4/3$ and a violation must overcome the slack first.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Sharp cap-breaking criterion]\label{thm:sharp}
For $0\le s\le 1$ and $\theta>0$,
\[
  \spd(s,\theta) > \frac43 \iff s(1-\theta) > \frac14 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since the cost is positive, $\spd>4/3$ is equivalent to $\cost(s,\theta)<3/4$, i.e.\
$1-s+s\theta<3/4$, i.e.\ $s(1-\theta)>1/4$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exchangeability never fires]\label{prop:amgm}
For all $\theta\in\R$, $\theta(1-\theta)\le 1/4$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\tfrac14-\theta(1-\theta) = (\theta-\tfrac12)^2 \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:cap} is thus Proposition~\ref{prop:amgm} read through
Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}: the cap law is the arithmetic--geometric mean inequality for a
pair of numbers summing to $1$.  It is not an artefact of the model's shape but the
impossibility of simultaneously retaining little and retaining the answer often, when
those are the same event.

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative escape cost]\label{thm:escape}
Let $0\le s\le 1$, $0<\theta<1$, and suppose $\spd(s,\theta)>4/3$.  Then
\[
  s-\theta \;>\; \frac{(1-2\theta)^2}{4(1-\theta)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp}, $s(1-\theta)>1/4$.  Multiplying the claim by $4(1-\theta)>0$,
it becomes $(1-2\theta)^2 < 4(1-\theta)(s-\theta)$.  Expanding,
\[
  4(1-\theta)(s-\theta) = 4s(1-\theta) - 4\theta(1-\theta) > 1 - 4\theta+4\theta^2
  = (1-2\theta)^2,
\]
using the criterion on the first term and Proposition~\ref{prop:amgm} on the second.
\end{proof}

The bound is a completed square over a positive quantity, hence nonnegative, and it
vanishes exactly at $\theta=1/2$.  Sample values of the required excess: $0.1778$ at
$\theta=0.1$; $0.0833$ at $\theta=0.25$; $0$ at $\theta=0.5$; and $1.6$ at $\theta=0.9$,
which is impossible since $s\le 1$.  Indeed by Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} the cap is
unreachable for every $\theta>3/4$, since then $s(1-\theta)\le 1-\theta<1/4$.

\begin{corollary}[Bias is necessary]\label{cor:bias}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:escape}, $\theta<s$: a dial beating the cap is
strictly more likely to retain the sought object than a random candidate.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Monotonicity in soundness]
For fixed $\theta\in(0,1]$, $\spd(s,\theta)$ is nondecreasing in $s$ on $[0,1]$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\cost(s,\theta)=1-s(1-\theta)$ is nonincreasing in $s$ since $1-\theta\ge 0$; inverting a
positive nonincreasing function gives a nondecreasing one.
\end{proof}

\section{The deterministic regime}

\begin{theorem}[Deterministic speedup]\label{thm:det}
A dial with soundness $s=1$ satisfies $\spd(1,\theta) = 1/\theta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\cost(1,\theta) = 1-1+\theta = \theta$.
\end{proof}

A dial that never discards the answer pays exactly the retained fraction, with no
correction term: its speedup is unbounded as $\theta\to 0^+$, and the cap phenomenon
evaporates.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:odd}
If $N$ is odd and $p\mid N$, then $p$ is odd.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If $2\mid p$ and $p\mid N$ then $2\mid N$, contradicting oddness.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The parity skip]\label{cor:parity}
The dial ``discard even candidates,'' applied to the divisor search for an odd $N$, has
$\theta=1/2$ and $s=1$, hence speedup exactly $2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:ratio}
At $\theta=1/2$, $\;\spd(1,\tfrac12)\,/\,\spd(\tfrac12,\tfrac12) = 2/(4/3) = 3/2$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The Berggren orbit and its universal residue table}

\begin{definition}
A triple $t=(a,b,c)\in\Z^3$ is \emph{Pythagorean} if $a^2+b^2=c^2$.  Define
\begin{align*}
  B_1(a,b,c) &= (a-2b+2c,\; 2a-b+2c,\; 2a-2b+3c),\\
  B_2(a,b,c) &= (a+2b+2c,\; 2a+b+2c,\; 2a+2b+3c),\\
  B_3(a,b,c) &= (-a+2b+2c,\; -2a+b+2c,\; -2a+2b+3c),
\end{align*}
and let $\mathcal{T}$ be the smallest set containing $(3,4,5)$ and closed under
$B_1,B_2,B_3$: the \emph{root component} of the Berggren tree.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}
Every $t\in\mathcal{T}$ is Pythagorean.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction.  The root satisfies $9+16=25$.  Each $B_i$ preserves the quadratic form
$a^2+b^2-c^2$ identically: expanding the image's defining identity reproduces the
hypothesis $a^2+b^2=c^2$ as a polynomial identity in $a,b,c$.
\end{proof}

Classically $\mathcal{T}$ is exactly the set of primitive Pythagorean triples with $a$
odd, each occurring once, arranged in a complete ternary tree.  We need only the
following.

\begin{theorem}[Congruence invariant of the root component]\label{thm:invariant}
For every $(a,b,c)\in\mathcal{T}$,
\[
  a\equiv 1\!\!\pmod 2,\qquad b\equiv 0\!\!\pmod 4,\qquad c\equiv 1\!\!\pmod 4 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induction.  The root $(3,4,5)$ satisfies all three.  Assume $(a,b,c)$ does and consider
$B_2$; the others differ only by signs, which do not affect the residue analysis beyond a
case split.
\begin{itemize}
  \item $a+2b+2c$: with $4\mid b$ we get $2b\equiv 0$ and $2c\equiv 2 \pmod 4$, so the
    coordinate is $a+2 \pmod 4$, odd because $a$ is.
  \item $2a+b+2c \equiv 2a+0+2 = 2(a+1) \pmod 4$, and $a$ odd makes $a+1$ even, so the
    coordinate is $\equiv 0 \pmod 4$.
  \item $2a+2b+3c \equiv 2a+0+3 \equiv 2+3 = 5 \equiv 1 \pmod 4$, using $a$ odd.
\end{itemize}
All cases are finite modular arithmetic and close the induction.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{revealed residue set mod $4$} of the root component is
\[
  R_4 = \bigl\{ (a\bmod 4,\, b \bmod 4,\, c\bmod 4) : (a,b,c)\in\mathcal{T}\bigr\}
  \subseteq (\Z/4)^3 .
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Universality]\label{thm:universal}
$R_4 = \bigl\{(1,0,1),\,(3,0,1)\bigr\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\subseteq$: by Theorem~\ref{thm:invariant}, $b\equiv 0$ and $c\equiv 1$ modulo $4$, and
$a$ odd means $a\equiv 1$ or $3$.  $\supseteq$: the root $(3,4,5)$ realises $(3,0,1)$, and
$B_1(3,4,5)=(5,12,13)$ realises $(1,0,1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No parameter dependence]\label{cor:noparam}
$R_4$ is a fixed two-element set: it depends on neither the search depth nor any target.
The orbit dial it induces is one universal exclusion table, computable in advance and
identical for every instance.
\end{corollary}

This is the central negative result of the characterisation.  The design premise under
test --- that the revealed subset varies with the target and hence carries per-target
information --- fails structurally, not for want of computational budget.  For the generic
non-root components the corresponding revealed sets saturate immediately to all residue
classes at every modulus considered, so again no per-target subset structure exists; only
frequencies and orbit lengths vary.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:three}
In every Pythagorean triple $(a,b,c)$, $3\mid ab$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Modulo $3$ the squares are $\{0,1\}$.  If neither $a$ nor $b$ is divisible by $3$ then
$a^2+b^2\equiv 2$, which is not a square modulo $3$.
\end{proof}

\section{Information content}

\begin{definition}
For a joint law $p$ on a finite product alphabet with marginals
$p_X(x)=\sum_y p(x,y)$ and $p_Y(y)=\sum_x p(x,y)$, the \emph{mutual information} is
\[
  I(X;Y) = \sum_{x,y} p(x,y)\log\frac{p(x,y)}{p_X(x)p_Y(y)},
\]
with $0\log 0 = 0$.  In bits, $I_{\mathrm{bits}} = I/\log 2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Gibbs: nonnegativity]\label{thm:gibbs}
For any nonnegative joint law $p$ of total mass $1$, $I(X;Y)\ge 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Cell by cell, with $r = p(x,y)/(p_X(x)p_Y(y))$ and $p(x,y)>0$, the bound
$\log r \ge 1 - 1/r$ gives
\[
  p(x,y)\log r \;\ge\; p(x,y)\Bigl(1-\tfrac1r\Bigr) = p(x,y)-p_X(x)p_Y(y).
\]
Cells with $p(x,y)=0$ satisfy the same bound trivially.  Summing, the right-hand side
telescopes to $1 - 1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Constant dials are uninformative]\label{thm:const}
If there is a symbol $y_0$ with $p(x,y)=0$ for all $x$ and all $y\ne y_0$, then
$I(X;Y)=0$ exactly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Then $p_X(x)=p(x,y_0)$ for every $x$, and $p_Y(y_0)=\sum_x p_X(x)=1$.  Cells with
$y\ne y_0$ contribute $0$; cells with $y=y_0$ and $p(x,y_0)>0$ have ratio $1$, hence
$\log 1 = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:floor}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:universal} the orbit dial's revealed table is a constant of the tree,
so its joint law with the target is of this form: it attains the absolute information
floor, $I=0$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:gibbs} no joint law achieves less.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}
Let $\mathcal{X}=\mathcal{Y}=\{0,1\}$ and $p(x,y)=\tfrac12$ if $x=y$, else $0$: a
perfectly correlated pair on two equiprobable classes.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:onebit}
For this law, $I(X;Y)=\log 2$: exactly one bit.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both marginals are uniform.  Each diagonal cell contributes
$\tfrac12\log\bigl(\tfrac{1/2}{1/4}\bigr)=\tfrac12\log 2$; off-diagonal cells contribute
$0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:resdial}
Let $p,q$ be odd with $pq\equiv 3 \pmod 4$.  Then exactly one of $p,q$ is $\equiv 1$ and
the other $\equiv 3$ modulo $4$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Each of $p,q$ is $1$ or $3$ mod $4$; the four products are $1,3,3,1$, so only the mixed
cases give $3$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zero information, super-cap speedup]\label{thm:paradox}
Assembling Theorems~\ref{thm:cap}, \ref{thm:det}, \ref{thm:const} and \ref{thm:onebit}:
the orbit dial has mutual information $0$ bits with the target; an ordinary residue dial
has $1$ bit; the orbit dial reads speedup $2$ at $\theta=1/2$; and every exchangeable dial
reads at most $4/3$.  In particular the zero-bit dial outperforms the ceiling binding the
one-bit dial: information does not order speedup.
\end{theorem}

\section{The resolution: prior, not information}\label{sec:dichotomy}

Soundness is not a property of a dial; it is a property of a dial \emph{relative to a
prior} over where the answer lies.

\begin{definition}
Fix a finite candidate pool $C$ and a \emph{fixed} dial, i.e.\ a retained set $K$.  Its
\emph{retention} is $\theta = |K\cap C|/|C|$.  Given a prior $\pi$ on $C$ with
$\sum_{a\in C}\pi(a)=1$, its \emph{soundness} is $s = \sum_{a\in K\cap C}\pi(a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform prior forces exchangeability]\label{thm:uniform}
If $\pi$ is uniform on $C$ then $s=\theta$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$s = \sum_{a\in K\cap C} 1/|C| = |K\cap C|/|C| = \theta$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:nolunch}
A fixed dial evaluated against a uniform prior obeys the $4/3$ cap.
\end{corollary}

This is the precise sense in which one cannot beat the cap without learning something: a
dial fixed in advance, facing a problem with no structural bias, is capped like everyone
else.  The loophole must therefore lie in the problem.

\begin{theorem}[Supported prior gives perfect soundness]\label{thm:supported}
If $\pi(a)=0$ for every $a\in C\setminus K$, then $s=1$, whatever the retention.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sum_{a\in K\cap C}\pi(a) = \sum_{a\in C}\pi(a) = 1$, since the omitted terms vanish.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Parity retention]\label{thm:parityret}
In the pool $C=\{0,1,\dots,2k-1\}$ the odd candidates number exactly $k$, so the parity
dial has retention exactly $1/2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$: adjoining $\{2k,2k+1\}$ adds exactly one odd element.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The escape, end to end]\label{thm:endtoend}
For odd $N$, in any even-length candidate pool, the parity dial has retention $1/2$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:parityret}) and soundness $1$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:odd} makes the
divisor prior supported in the odd residues, then Theorem~\ref{thm:supported}), hence
speedup $2$; whereas the same fixed dial against a uniform prior has soundness $1/2$ and
speedup exactly $4/3$.
\end{theorem}

\paragraph{The dichotomy.}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:bias}, beating the cap requires $s>\theta$.  By
Theorems~\ref{thm:uniform} and~\ref{thm:supported} there are exactly two sources of such a
gap.  \emph{Information}: the dial's kept set varies with the instance, correlated with
the answer's location --- this is the regime the cap law governs, capped at $4/3$.
\emph{Prior concentration}: the kept set is fixed, but the answer was already constrained
to lie inside it by a congruence true of the problem class rather than of the instance ---
this regime carries zero bits and escapes the cap.  The orbit dial is entirely of the
second kind.  The correct statement of the barrier is therefore: \textbf{no
dial with no answer-bias cannot beat $4/3$}; a super-cap reading certifies bias, and only
the \emph{source} of the bias distinguishes a discovery from a blind constant.

\paragraph{The filter accounting rule.}
An informational claim must be measured on the pool with the blind structure already
discounted.  A dial that quietly re-derives a structural congruence inherits its constant
and can read above the cap while contributing nothing of its own.  Discount the blind part
--- measure retention and soundness relative to the already-structurally-filtered pool ---
and only a genuine instance-dependent bias survives.  In the readings below nothing
survived: discounted, the orbit dial reads exactly $1$.

\section{Wheels: the unbounded structural family}

\begin{definition}
Fix a squarefree modulus $M$ coprime to the target $N$.  The \emph{wheel dial of modulus
$M$} retains exactly the candidates coprime to $M$.  Its idealised speedup is
$W(M) = M/\varphi(M)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness]
If $\gcd(N,M)=1$ and $p\mid N$, then $\gcd(p,M)=1$; hence $s=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A common factor of $p$ and $M$ divides both $N$ and $M$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Retention]
Exactly $\varphi(M)$ of the $M$ residues mod $M$ are coprime to $M$, so the retained
fraction is $\varphi(M)/M$ and, by Theorem~\ref{thm:det}, the speedup is $M/\varphi(M)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Blindness]
The kept set $\{p:\gcd(p,M)=1\}$ does not depend on $N$: one universal table per modulus,
carrying zero bits by Theorem~\ref{thm:const}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Euler product]\label{thm:euler}
For $M=\prod_{p\in S}p$ with $S$ a finite set of distinct primes,
$W(M) = \prod_{p\in S} \frac{p}{p-1}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Multiplicativity of $\varphi$ on coprime factors and $\varphi(p)=p-1$ give
$\varphi(M)=\prod_{p\in S}(p-1)$ by induction on $S$; divide termwise into
$M=\prod_{p\in S}p$.
\end{proof}

Concretely $W(2)=2$, $W(6)=3$, $W(30)=15/4$, $W(210)=35/8$.  All exceed $4/3$; all carry
zero bits.

\begin{theorem}[Structural dials are unbounded]\label{thm:unbounded}
For every $B\in\R$ there is a squarefree $M$ with $W(M)>B$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two ingredients.  First, the Weierstrass bound $\prod_i(1+x_i)\ge 1+\sum_i x_i$ for
$x_i\ge 0$, proved by induction (the step needs only $x_a\sum_i x_i \ge 0$).  Second, for
prime $p$, $\frac{p}{p-1}\ge 1+\frac1p$, equivalent after clearing denominators to
$p-\frac1p\ge p-1$.  Combining with Theorem~\ref{thm:euler},
$W(M)\ge 1+\sum_{p\in S}1/p$.  Since $\sum_p 1/p$ diverges, choose a finite $S$ with
$\sum_{p\in S}1/p>B$.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:unbounded} is decisive: \textbf{the $4/3$ cap is not a bound on
speedups.}  Information-free filters of arbitrarily large constant advantage exist --- the
classical wheels --- and their advantage is limited only by the ($\log\log$-slow, but
unbounded) divergence of $\sum 1/p$.

\subsection{Tropical accounting}

Independent structural dials compose multiplicatively, so their logarithms add: the
arithmetic of the tropical semiring $(\R\cup\{+\infty\},\min,+)$, where ``multiplication''
is addition of weights and ``addition'' is minimum.

\begin{theorem}[Structural composition]
$\spd(1,\theta_1\theta_2) = \spd(1,\theta_1)\,\spd(1,\theta_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(\theta_1\theta_2)^{-1} = \theta_1^{-1}\theta_2^{-1}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:det}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Wheel multiplicativity]
For coprime $M_1,M_2$, $W(M_1M_2) = W(M_1)W(M_2)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{tropical weight} of a wheel is $w(M) = \log W(M)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}
$w$ is a monoid homomorphism on coprime moduli: $w(M_1M_2)=w(M_1)+w(M_2)$, i.e.\ tropical
multiplication of weights.  Tropical addition is
$w(M_1)\oplus w(M_2) = \min\bigl(w(M_1),w(M_2)\bigr)$, the pessimistic combination.
Moreover $w(M)\ge 0$ for $M\ge 1$, since $\varphi(M)\le M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded structural window]
$\sup_M w(M) = +\infty$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:unbounded} with bound $e^B$ and take logarithms.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded exchangeable window]
For $\theta\in(0,1]$, $\log\spd(\theta,\theta)\in[0,\log\tfrac43]$, and the window is
closed under composition: if $\theta_1,\theta_2\in(0,1]$ the stacked dial with retention
$\theta_1\theta_2$ is again exchangeable and again capped.
\end{theorem}

\paragraph{The scope note in tropical form.}
Exchangeable weights inhabit the compact window $[0,\log(4/3)]\approx[0,0.2877]$, closed
under composition.  Structural weights form an unbounded additive family, with the parity
skip at $w(2)=\log 2 \approx 0.6931$ --- already outside the window, with zero bits of
instance content.  These are two genuinely different objects, not two points on one scale.

\section{Experimental readings}

The theory was tested on a population of $800$ semiprimes of bit-length $40$ ($500$
balanced, $300$ with factor ratio $4$), moduli $m\in\{3,4,5,7,8,16\}$, permutation nulls
stratified by decile of $\log N$, at the extremal operating point $\theta=1/2$ under
$\sqrt{\cdot}$-descending trial division.

\paragraph{Matched-random arm.}
A dial retaining a random half of the candidates read $1.3387$ with confidence interval
$[1.3008,\,1.382]$, against the prediction $4/3=1.3333$ of Theorems~\ref{thm:cap}
and~\ref{thm:extremal}: the cap law confirmed to within about $0.4\%$.  A co-inflation
control designed to detect artefactual enhancement returned $[1.2717,\,1.3579]$, cleanly
bracketing the prediction.

\paragraph{Orbit arm.}
The orbit dial read $2.0000$ with interval $[2.0,\,2.0]$ and failure rate $0.000$,
matching Corollary~\ref{cor:parity} exactly.  Compared instance by instance against a
universal fixed dial computable from $N$ alone with no tree walk, the paired difference
was $0.0$ \emph{exactly}: the orbit dial \emph{is} the parity skip, and the observed
$2\times$ is a constant shave, not a barrier event.  The measured ratio between the arms,
$2/1.3387 = 1.494$, matches the theoretical $3/2$ of Corollary~\ref{cor:ratio} to within
the matched-random arm's own error.

\paragraph{Information null.}
Across $48$ measurement cells the maximum standardised joint deviation was $+2.29$ and the
maximum conditional deviation $+1.78$ --- the extremes expected from $48$ draws under the
null.  No feature carried more than $0.09$ bits, against ordinary residue baselines of
$I(N\bmod m;\,p\bmod m) = 1.00$--$3.00$ bits, exactly as Theorem~\ref{thm:const} and
Corollary~\ref{cor:noparam} predict.

\paragraph{Loaded accounting.}
When each arm was charged its own evaluation overhead, every arm read below $1$ (range
$0.20$--$0.75$).  The idealised accounting above charges nothing for applying a dial; in
practice the bookkeeping of a large wheel eventually consumes the constant it buys.  This
is the honest practical caveat, and the reason none of these constants yield a usable
factoring advantage at cryptographic sizes.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the cap does and does not say}

The cap law is often stated informally as ``filters are worth at most $4/3$.''  That is
false, and the parity skip refutes it in one line.  The correct statement is conditional
on exchangeability, which is itself conditional on the prior:

\begin{quote}
A filter whose retention of the answer is statistically indistinguishable from its
retention of an arbitrary candidate cannot be worth more than $4/3$, whatever it retains.
A filter that beats $4/3$ has an answer-retention bias, sourced either from instance
information or from a structural congruence of the problem class.
\end{quote}

The second sentence is the contribution: it converts a barrier into a classification.

\subsection{Why the loaded arms all read below one}

Charging $\kappa$ per candidate for evaluating the dial turns the cost into
$\kappa+1-s+s\theta$.  Immediately the exchangeable cap tightens to $1/(\kappa+3/4)$, and
the deterministic parity dial falls below $1$ once $\kappa>1/2$.  This one-parameter
deformation appears to account deterministically for the observed loaded readings.

\subsection{Relation to sieve practice}

Everything in the wheel section is, mathematically, the wheel of a classical sieve; the
contribution is not the wheel but its \emph{classification} as a zero-information object,
together with the observation that the divergence of $\sum 1/p$ is precisely the statement
that this class is unbounded.  The tropical framing then makes the two regimes'
incomparability structural rather than numerical: one is a bounded interval closed under
the semiring operation, the other an unbounded submonoid.

\subsection{The methodological moral}

Whenever a heuristic accelerates a search, one may ask which of two things happened: it
\emph{learned} something about this instance, or it \emph{exploited} something true of
every instance.  The two are indistinguishable from timing data alone, and are cleanly
separated by a single control: recompute the filter's kept set with the instance withheld.
If the table is unchanged, the advantage is structural, bounded by the problem's
symmetries, and worth exactly its constant.  If the table moves, the advantage is
informational, and the cap applies.  The paired-difference-of-zero reported above is
exactly this control firing.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Loaded cap law]
Charge the dial its own evaluation cost $\kappa$ per candidate.  Then the loaded speedup
$1/(\kappa+1-s+s\theta)$ obeys the strictly smaller exchangeable cap $1/(\kappa+3/4)$, and
there is a critical overhead $\kappa^\ast = 1/2$ beyond which even the deterministic
parity dial ($s=1$, $\theta=1/2$) falls below $1$.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that the loaded arms reading below $1$ are not noise but the
deterministic image of a single one-parameter cost identity.  The unloaded law being a
completed square, the loaded statement changes only the constant term.

\begin{conjecture}[Mertens ceiling for blind structural sieving]
Along primorials, $\log\bigl(M/\varphi(M)\bigr) = \log\log M + \gamma + o(1)$, so the
tropical weight of any information-free dial applicable in time $T$ is at most
$\log\log T + O(1)$.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that tropical accounting turns the Euler product into a partial sum of
$1/p$, and Mertens' theorem is exactly the growth law of that sum.  The payoff would be a
second barrier complementary to the first: unbiased dials capped at $4/3$
absolutely, structural dials capped at $\log\log$ of the available time.

\paragraph{Further questions.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Partial soundness.}  The dichotomy is stated for priors either uniform or
    fully supported in the kept set.  What is the right statement for intermediate priors,
    and does the escape margin interpolate with the prior's overlap with $K$?
  \item \emph{Optimal stacking under load.}  Given per-dial overheads $\kappa_i$ and
    structural weights $w_i$, which subset of wheels maximises the loaded speedup?  The
    tropical framing suggests a knapsack in log-coordinates with an additive penalty.
  \item \emph{Adaptive dials.}  A multi-stage adaptive dial, whose later stages depend on
    earlier failures, is not exchangeable even if each stage is.  Whether the $4/3$ cap
    survives adaptivity, or degrades with stage count, is open.
  \item \emph{Other structural families.}  Beyond wheels, which congruence families give
    sound blind exclusions with computable retention?  Theorem~\ref{thm:three} supplies
    one; a systematic classification would make the ``structural budget'' of a search
    problem an intrinsic invariant.
\end{itemize}

\section{Conclusion}

In the model $\cost(s,\theta)=1-s+s\theta$: exchangeable dials are capped at $4/3$,
attained exactly at half-retention; the cap breaks exactly when $s(1-\theta)>1/4$, with
quantified escape cost $s-\theta>(1-2\theta)^2/(4(1-\theta))$; deterministic dials achieve
$1/\theta$, unbounded; the Berggren triple tree's revealed residue table is the fixed set
$\{(1,0,1),(3,0,1)\}$ mod $4$, hence carries exactly zero bits --- the absolute
information floor; yet the dial it induces reads $2>4/3$, because the divisors of an odd
number are already odd, so the escape is created by the prior, not by information; and
this structural regime is unbounded, by divergence of $\sum 1/p$, with log-speedups
composing tropically while the exchangeable regime stays confined to $[0,\log\tfrac43]$.

The barrier stands, with its scope now delimited: \textbf{it bounds dials carrying no bias
toward the answer; blind structural exclusions escape it for free, carrying zero bits and
worth exactly their constant factor, and nothing more.}

